⸻

3. PRIMARY TARGET: CONTROLLER FEEL

The player should notice the improvement almost immediately after touching the controls.

Currently ALVA already has acceleration, deceleration, turn boosting, air acceleration, coyote time and jump buffering.

Build upon those systems rather than throwing them away.

Movement should have three simultaneous qualities:

Immediate

The fighter responds quickly when the player gives an input.

Smooth

Velocity should transition naturally instead of looking like the fighter is snapping between unrelated states.

Precise

The player must still be capable of small positioning corrections.

Do not confuse responsiveness with excessive speed.

⸻

4. GROUND MOVEMENT

Improve the relationship between:

* acceleration
* maximum speed
* deceleration
* reversing
* animation
* attacks

Starting to move should feel immediate.

Reversing direction should be responsive without instantly teleporting velocity from full left to full right.

Use a strong but controlled turn acceleration.

The fighter should have enough acceleration to make close-range spacing practical.

Stopping should happen quickly enough that the player can intentionally position beside an opponent.

Avoid excessive ground sliding after releasing movement.

At the same time, do not completely destroy momentum.

There should be a short, natural-feeling deceleration rather than an artificial instant stop.

Desired result

The player should be able to perform:

run right → stop beside opponent → BA1

without constantly overshooting.

They should also be able to perform:

run right → reverse left → immediately reposition

without waiting for a sluggish turnaround.

⸻

5. AIR MOVEMENT

Air control is one of the most important parts of this rework.

ALVA should feel substantially better to steer after jumping.

The player should be able to:

* chase an opponent after launching them
* correct a jump that was slightly misplaced
* drift toward or away from an opponent
* reverse aerial direction with reasonable responsiveness
* position an aerial attack intentionally

Do not make aerial movement identical to ground movement.

The fighter should retain momentum.

Air acceleration should influence velocity rather than simply replacing it.

Opposite-direction input should provide enough air braking/turning force that aerial positioning feels intentional.

The result should feel permissive without making trajectory meaningless.

⸻

6. JUMP FEEL

Preserve ALVA’s existing jump ability and Jump Power system.

Improve the FEEL surrounding the jump.

Keep and refine:

* jump buffering
* coyote time

Make takeoff feel immediate.

Ensure horizontal velocity carries naturally into the jump.

Running before jumping should produce a natural forward-moving jump.

A standing jump should remain controllable.

Avoid arbitrary horizontal speed resets when entering the jump state.

The player should feel continuous motion:

run → jump → aerial movement

rather than:

run → separate jump state.

⸻

7. ADD FAST FALL AS A MOVEMENT QUALITY TOOL

Implement a simple fast-fall if it integrates cleanly with ALVA.

This is not being added “because MultiVersus has fast falling.”

It is useful because ALVA needs better vertical positioning and faster return-to-neutral.

When airborne and already descending, holding down should increase downward velocity toward a controlled fast-fall maximum.

Requirements:

* cannot trigger while rising
* should not teleport velocity
* accelerates smoothly
* should be immediately understandable
* should make aerial combat less floaty
* should allow players to intentionally return to the ground after an aerial attack
* should work with platform interaction cleanly

Expose the relevant values through character movement data rather than scattering magic constants throughout the fighter state machine.

⸻

8. MOMENTUM MUST SURVIVE ATTACKS

This is critical.

Normal attacks should not automatically make movement feel disconnected.

Review the current lockMovement behavior and attack state handling.

Separate the concepts of:

* cannot freely accelerate
* cannot move at all
* retain existing momentum
* attack applies its own movement

These should NOT all mean the same thing.

For most normal attacks, entering an attack should preserve some existing velocity.

Example:

running → BA1

should not feel as though an invisible wall instantly stopped the character unless the attack intentionally requires that behavior.

Likewise:

jumping forward → air BA1

should retain meaningful forward drift.

The attack can reduce steering while active without deleting momentum.

⸻

9. ATTACK MOBILITY

Give attacks individual movement behavior where appropriate.

Extend attack definitions with optional data-driven movement properties rather than hardcoding behavior for #0001.

Possible schema:

movementControl

* amount of normal steering retained during attack

momentumMultiplier

* modifies existing horizontal momentum when attack starts

activeMovement

* optional velocity/impulse generated by the attack

airControl

* attack-specific aerial steering multiplier

These names are suggestions; use the architecture that fits the project best.

Examples:

BA1:

* keeps some forward momentum
* little additional movement
* recovers quickly

BA2:

* slightly more commitment
* can have subtle forward movement during the strike
* longer recovery than BA1

Air BA1:

* preserves aerial drift
* retains moderate steering
* should be useful while pursuing airborne opponents

Air BA2:

* retains horizontal momentum
* its downward attack identity remains intact
* should not arbitrarily freeze the fighter horizontally

Shuriken:

* should not feel like the character becomes rooted to the floor longer than necessary

Do not change the identity of the attacks.

Improve how they coexist with movement.

⸻

10. COMBOS ARE A MAJOR GOAL

ALVA’s attacks should intentionally support short combos.

The player should not need frame-perfect execution.

The game should recognize that after landing certain attacks, the attacker is supposed to have an opportunity to follow up.

This does NOT mean every attack should combo into everything.

Create intentional routes.

Target primarily:

* 2-hit combos
* 3-hit combos
* occasional longer sequences created through positioning or charged abilities

Avoid designing infinite combos.

⸻

11. WHY COMBOS CURRENTLY FEEL DIFFICULT

Inspect the interaction between:

* attack recovery
* cooldown
* hitstop
* target hitstun
* launch velocity
* attacker movement
* air acceleration
* input timing
* attack startup
* hitbox position
* knockback scaling through Launch Point

Do not solve combos using only longer hitstun.

For a combo to feel good:

1. the first hit must place the opponent somewhere useful;
2. the attacker must recover soon enough;
3. the attacker must be able to move into follow-up range;
4. the next attack must start quickly enough;
5. the opponent must have a fair eventual escape.

Treat all five as one system.

⸻

12. INTENTIONAL COMBO ROUTES

Tune #0001 so several understandable routes are possible.

Exact balance should be determined through testing, but the desired relationships include routes such as:

BA1 → BA1

At low Launch Point and appropriate spacing, a successful light attack should sometimes allow another light attack.

Do not make this an endless loop.

Pushback, hitstun, attack recovery and accumulated Launch Point should naturally end the sequence.

⸻

BA1 → BA2

This should be one of the basic grounded combo routes.

BA1 creates the opening.

BA2 acts as the stronger follow-up and launcher.

The player should have enough time to input BA2 without requiring near-frame-perfect execution.

⸻

BA1 → jump → air BA1

This should reward pursuit.

After connecting the opening attack, the player can jump after the opponent and continue pressure.

Movement must be responsive enough to make this practical.

⸻

BA2 → aerial follow-up

Since BA2 launches vertically, the attacker should have a reasonable opportunity at lower Launch Point to jump and pursue.

Possible follow-ups:

* air BA1
* air BA2 depending on positioning

The Launch Point system should eventually make the target travel too far for the same sequence to remain guaranteed.

That naturally prevents repetitive permanent combos.

⸻

Air BA1 → aerial/ground continuation

If the attacker lands appropriately after an aerial hit, recovery and landing behavior should allow pressure to continue without an awkward dead period.

⸻

Air BA2 → grounded pressure

Because air BA2 drives the target downward, landing it near the stage should potentially create a grounded continuation.

Preserve its downward-launch identity.

⸻

13. DO NOT MAKE COMBOS FRAME-PERFECT

Add a small combat input buffer.

This is extremely important.

If the player presses an attack slightly before their current attack/recovery finishes, remember that input briefly.

When the fighter becomes actionable, execute the buffered action if it is still valid.

Use a small window approximately in the range of:

80–150 ms

and tune through testing.

The exact final value should feel forgiving without allowing ancient inputs to fire unexpectedly.

Buffer:

* normal combat actions
* jump where appropriate

Do not blindly buffer every possible control.

Existing jump buffering should integrate cleanly with this system.

⸻

14. HITSTUN AND RECOVERY

Rebalance attack recovery relative to hitstun.

For attacks intended to start combos:

attacker recovery should finish sufficiently before the target fully recovers that the attacker has time to reposition and attempt the intended follow-up.

Do NOT simply give every attack enormous hitstun.

Different attacks should have different roles.

For example:

BA1:
quick startup
short recovery
moderate hitstun
low/moderate launch

BA2:
more startup
more commitment
stronger launch
primarily launcher/finisher

Air BA1:
pursuit/combo tool

Air BA2:
downward positioning/finisher tool

Shuriken:
spacing/interruption tool rather than the universal combo starter

⸻

15. LAUNCH POINT MUST NATURALLY CHANGE COMBOS

Preserve ALVA’s Launch Point system.

It should actually help solve combo scaling.

At low Launch Point:

* opponents remain relatively close
* basic follow-ups are practical

At medium Launch Point:

* routes require better movement/readjustment
* fewer sequences remain guaranteed

At high Launch Point:

* attacks send opponents far enough that many ordinary combos naturally stop
* combat becomes more about pursuit and ring-outs

Do not introduce an unrelated combo percentage system.

Use ALVA’s existing Launch Point mechanics.

⸻

16. HIT FEEL

Improve impact without making gameplay sluggish.

Preserve ALVA’s existing hitstop architecture.

Tune hitstop per attack according to strength.

Light attacks:
short, crisp freeze

Heavy attacks:
slightly stronger freeze

Large Sphere Rush explosion:
strongest impact

Avoid excessive freeze on repeated light attacks.

Hitstop should communicate:

“I connected.”

It should not make controls feel delayed.

⸻

17. ATTACK INPUT FEEL

An attack button should feel responsive even when the character cannot execute the attack on that exact simulation step.

Use the combat input buffer described earlier.

If:

BA1 is recovering

and the player presses BA2 shortly before BA1 ends,

then BA2 should begin on the earliest legal actionable frame, provided the buffered input remains valid.

This will dramatically improve perceived responsiveness and combo accessibility without requiring attacks themselves to become unrealistically fast.

⸻

18. LANDING AFTER AERIAL ATTACKS

Inspect aerial attack → landing transitions carefully.

Do not create unnecessary landing lockout.

When an aerial attack has already completed and the fighter lands, they should transition naturally back to grounded control.

If an attack is still legitimately in recovery when landing, preserve the remaining commitment.

Do not arbitrarily restart a complete recovery period solely because the character touched the ground.

The animation/state machine should reflect gameplay state rather than imposing additional invisible punishment.

⸻

19. FACING

Facing should update naturally during neutral movement.

Attacks should generally snapshot/lock the relevant attack direction once their committed strike begins so hitboxes do not unpredictably flip halfway through a move.

However, movement immediately before attacking should make turning attacks feel responsive.

The sequence:

run left → quickly reverse → BA1

should reliably attack toward the player’s intended side.

Avoid stale facing.

⸻

20. DASH

Keep ALVA’s existing Dash concept.

Do NOT automatically turn it into MultiVersus’s dodge system.

Improve its feel within ALVA.

The Dash should:

* begin immediately after valid input
* have a clear burst
* preserve sensible momentum when ending
* transition smoothly back into ordinary movement
* retain its Energy cost
* remain grounded unless ALVA’s design is deliberately expanded later

Review the current behavior where normal acceleration resumes from dash velocity.

Tune the handoff so it feels smooth rather than creating an awkward velocity spike or abrupt brake.

Do not add attack-cancel-out-of-dash solely because the reference game has it.

If allowing attacks near the end of Dash materially improves ALVA, implement it only as an ALVA-specific quality-of-life window and test it carefully.

⸻

21. SHIELD

Preserve ALVA’s Shield system.

Shield should remain:

* held Defense
* Energy-based
* capable of blocking attacks
* governed by existing blockstun rules

Improve only transitions into and out of Shield where necessary.

Releasing Shield should return control cleanly.

Do not recreate MultiVersus’s defensive system.

⸻

22. CHARGE

Preserve Charge exactly as an important ALVA mechanic.

Charge must continue to:

* accelerate Energy regeneration
* accelerate charged-action cooldown recovery
* enable Charged BA1
* enable Charged BA2

Do not make the new movement/combo system accidentally bypass Charge’s role.

⸻

23. CLONE ATTACK

Preserve Charged BA1’s existing behavior.

The clone remains independent after being summoned.

Its ability to attack from behind or use the aerial fallback should remain.

The movement rework must not break:

* clone spawn positioning
* clone facing
* clone hitboxes
* clone cloud animation
* cooldown
* detached hit behavior

The player’s improved movement should simply make it easier to capitalize on clone pressure.

⸻

24. SPHERE RUSH

Preserve Sphere Rush’s identity and phase structure:

formation
→ rush
→ contact
→ hold/ticks
→ explosion
→ recovery

Do not apply ordinary attack-movement rules to Sphere Rush.

Sphere Rush already owns its movement through the charged-technique system.

Keep that separation.

Ensure the broader movement changes do not alter its authored rush speed, ground requirement, binding behavior or explosion behavior unintentionally.

⸻

25. DATA-DRIVEN IMPLEMENTATION

Avoid #0001-specific hacks inside Fighter.update().

If attacks need different movement characteristics, represent them in attack definitions.

If movement needs new tunables, add them to the movement definition.

Potential movement properties could include:

* fastFallAcceleration
* fastFallSpeed
* attackMomentumRetention
* combatInputBuffer

Potential attack properties could include:

* movementControl
* airMovementControl
* momentumMultiplier
* movementImpulse

Use sensible names consistent with the existing codebase.

Provide defaults so future fighters do not require every property.

⸻

26. ANIMATION MUST FOLLOW PHYSICS

Do not use animation frames to physically drag the fighter around unless a move explicitly owns movement.

The physics/state system is authoritative.

Animation communicates what physics is doing.

Maintain ALVA’s existing sprite normalization and bottom-center anchoring.

Do not change sprite dimensions to fake movement.

Do not alter artwork.

⸻

27. TARGET FEEL FOR #0001

The finished #0001 should feel like an agile fighter.

Not weightless.

Not slippery.

Not hyperactive.

The ideal sensation is:

“I pressed it and he responded.”

“I can correct my movement.”

“I can chase after my hit.”

“My attack didn’t randomly kill all my momentum.”

“I can tell when my next attack is available.”

“I pressed the next attack slightly early and the game understood me.”

“I can actually perform the combo I intended.”

“I can improvise when positioning changes.”

⸻

28. TEST SPECIFIC SCENARIOS

Do not finish after changing constants.

Play/test these scenarios individually.

Movement test

Repeatedly:

stand
→ run
→ stop
→ reverse
→ jump
→ reverse in air
→ land

All transitions should feel controlled.

Microspacing test

Try to stop immediately outside BA1 range.

Then move slightly forward and attack.

This should be practical rather than frustrating.

Running attack test

run
→ BA1

Momentum should transition naturally into the attack.

Aerial pursuit test

BA2
→ jump toward opponent
→ air BA1

At low Launch Point this should be realistically executable.

Basic combo test

BA1
→ BA2

This should be reliable at appropriate low Launch Point and spacing.

Buffered combo test

Press BA2 slightly before BA1 recovery ends.

The input should execute when BA2 becomes legal.

Air combo test

jump
→ air BA1
→ reposition
→ follow-up

The fighter should remain steerable enough for this to feel intentional.

Downward attack test

air BA2
→ opponent driven downward
→ land
→ attempt grounded continuation

Transitions should remain smooth.

High Launch Point test

Repeat combo attempts against a target with high Launch Point.

The increased launch should naturally break routes that worked at low Launch Point.

This is desired.

Shield test

attack
→ recover
→ Shield

and

Shield
→ release
→ move/attack

Neither transition should feel unnecessarily delayed.

Dash test

run
→ Dash
→ movement

The handoff back into normal locomotion should be smooth.

